e  in the 1980s was lossy based compression  as the name suggests this means that  part of the data is lost during  compression for example if you were  compressing a 4k image you could  substantially reduce the size of the  file by having its resolution but this  is obviously not the best solution  eugene's goal was to create a lossless  data compression algorithm and this led  him to creating the rar file format but  wait a minute how does lossless data  compression even work how can you store  the same data within a smaller space  without losing any data well the key is  to leverage natural redundancies within  the data itself for example let's take a  piece of text if you wanted to save this  code onto your computer the most  rudimentary method would be to go letter  by letter you could tell the computer  that the first character is i and the  second character is t and the third  character is a space and so on and so  forth but a far more efficient method  would be to tell the computer that the  character in the 1st 17th 23rd 31st and  34th positions are all the letter i  now of course that's an extremely basic  form of data compression but that's the  overall premise the idea is to eliminate  redundant data storage as much as  possible by using algorithms machine  learning and partial matching etc  anyway after grinding for quite some  time eugene came up with a new file  format called rawr 1993 and two years  later he would release a piece of  software that could compress data into  it and out of the raw file format and  this marked the birth of winrar given  that the software was free to try it  didn't take long for winrar to gain  popularity within the computer market  after all most users were desperate for  storage this isn't nearly as relatable  nowadays given that we have loads of  storage not to mention it external  storage options as well as cloud storage  options are both cheaper than ever and  this is one of the main reasons that the  popularity of w