 software would consider pirating it  clearly this would not be a very  favorable outcome and it's more than  enough motivation for companies to  simply pay for all their software  in the end winrar was basically the  godfather of the freemium business model  just get users on your side and then  figure out monetization later when they  first leveraged this model in the 1990s  it was quite a bold move but today many  of the businesses that we come across on  a daily basis use the same model i mean  when was the last time that you paid for  instagram or facebook or google or  snapchat none of these companies have a  pop-up window that tells you that the  service is not free and that you have to  pay for it but at the core they all make  money in similar manners once they have  you on their side they offer some sort  of service to companies and it's the  companies that pay for you nowadays this  model has actually become so popular  that it's unique if you don't leverage  it for example the selling point of some  new search engines and social media  platforms is that they don't collect  your data they're either decentralized  nonprofit organizations or they directly  charge customers for their use youtube  premium is a perfect example of this it  doesn't look like winrar is trying to  hop onto this new trend though they've  become iconic for their 40 day draw  period that's actually infinite and i  don't think they're looking to change  that also based on google trends it  looks like winrar is on its way out  that's the demand for file compression  amongst everyday people is slowly dying  off according to this website they still  pull in 21 million worth of revenue and  to give in that they run an extremely  lean operation and they're likely still  profiting millions per year  so while they probably won't become the  next adobe it looks like they've got us  all the business that works for them and  that won't be changing anytime soon do  you guys still use winrar comm